<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
<div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
<a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
<span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
<span class="text-cyan-100/90">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</span>
</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
<p class="badge">Shopify buying guide</p>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Klaviyo vs Omnisend for Shopify SMS</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Compare data requirements, safe workflow edits and ongoing maintenance in each current account. This guide places both candidates against twelve adjacent products, including a retired messaging suite kept only for migration context so the evaluation reflects audience, permission scope, catalog, team and channel mix. Data-depth and speed-to-live rankings are hypotheses to test, not documented results.</p>
<p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">Claims below are fit judgments, not vendor promises. Features, pricing, SMS availability, and carrier rules change. Follow official links, ask for written commercial terms, and run the same permissioned pilot in each finalist.</p>
</header>

    <div class="mt-10 card overflow-x-auto">
<h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Decision table</h2>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Question</th>
<th>Klaviyo</th>
<th>Omnisend</th>
<th>Practical tie-breaker</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
      <tr>
<td>Best starting point</td>
<td>Shopify events and profile segments</td>
<td>Shared email/SMS/push builder</td>
<td>Who owns weekly flow maintenance?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SMS role</td>
<td>Email and SMS flow evaluation</td>
<td>Email, SMS and push evaluation</td>
<td>Model delivered messages and carrier fees</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Likely friction</td>
<td>Profile growth and setup depth</td>
<td>Channel eligibility and queue edits</td>
<td>Test suppression, not demo screens</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Commercial caveat</td>
<td>Active-profile plus usage economics</td>
<td>Contacts, sends, and channels</td>
<td>Request a 12-month projection</td>
</tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>

    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Current scope and primary sources</h2>
<p>Scope checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo lifecycle notice</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo migration announcement</a>, <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/">Firepush web push</a>, <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">Postscript Email launch and access invitation</a>, and <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Sequenzy Shopify scope</a>. Confirm proposed account access and contracts before selecting a provider. Card order is not a ranking; Yotpo messaging is excluded from live shortlists.</p>
<h2>How to interpret this comparison</h2>
<p>Shopify SMS software is a permissioned messaging system, not a shorter email editor. The useful comparison is whether a tool can capture and preserve consent, recognize Shopify events, enforce quiet hours and exclusions, make message cost visible, and tell an operator what happened after a send. A feature checkbox does not prove any of those things works in your store.</p>
<p>Use the same requirements for event naming, segment ownership, deliverability and experiment design in both accounts. Verify which prebuilt journeys and channels are available in the proposed plan and market rather than assuming a safer default. Either candidate can miss the actual requirement if permissioned capture, reply handling or an unowned suppression process is the unresolved problem.</p>
<h2>A pair-specific decision rule</h2>
<p>Choose Klaviyo if the required Shopify event and profile segments can be maintained there with fewer manual repairs in your proposed account. Choose Omnisend if the shared email/SMS/push path and templates reduce operator work while passing the same exclusions. Both are positive product reasons to evaluate, not proven rankings. Keep the existing platform if neither candidate offers a material operational or economic improvement.</p>
<h2>What changes the answer</h2>
<p>Start with message jobs: cart recovery, back-in-stock, launch access, replenishment, service notification and broad promotion. Establish permission for each purpose and channel instead of treating a phone number or email address as universal authorization. Check current purchase, subscription, opt-out and suppression state at dispatch. A timely reminder still needs a valid event, an accountable sender and a queue that can cancel stale messages.</p>
<p>Then price the whole program. Include platform fee, contact or profile tier, email volume, SMS segments, carrier surcharges, MMS, discounts, creative labor, migration time, and the cost of opt-outs or complaints. “Revenue attributed” is not contribution margin and is not incremental lift. Keep a holdout wherever the platform makes that possible.</p>
</article>

    <h2 class="mt-14 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-white">14 products: live evaluation roles and retired messaging context</h2>
<div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">{tools.map((tool, index) => <article class="card">
<p class="text-sm font-black text-cyan-200">{index + 1}. {tool.best}</p>
